{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader lord who played a significant role in the history of the County of Tripoli during the medieval period. Born around 1140 he was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III inherited the title of Count of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1152 making him one of the youngest rulers in the Crusader states of Outremer.\n\n*highlighted section part 1: Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III's early life was marked by the challenges and political intrigues common in the Crusader states. As a minor his regency was initially handled by his mother Hodierna and her lover or husband William of Bures. William's dominance and brutal rule led to widespread resentment and in 1157 Raymond who was around 17 years old took control of his county. He immediately repudiated his mother and William and set about consolidating his authority.\n\nWith the support of the military orders and the local nobles Raymond III began to rebuild his county's strength and prestige. His rule was characterized by a mix of diplomacy and military action as he sought to defend his territories against both Muslim and Christian threats. During his early years Raymond III faced challenges from the Artuqid dynasty to the north and the growing power of the Zengid dynasty to the east.\n\n*highlighted section part 2: Military Campaigns and Alliances*\nRaymond III's reign saw him engage in several important military campaigns. In 1163 he led a crusading army against the Zengid ruler Nur ad-Din. The siege of Harim ended in failure when a relief force arrived and Raymond was forced to retreat. However this campaign demonstrated his willingness to take on powerful enemies and his commitment to the defense of the Crusader states.\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III allied himself with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos to launch a joint expedition against the Fatimid Caliphate in Egypt. The campaign ended in disaster after the death of Manuel's fleet in a storm and the loss of much of the army to disease. Despite these setbacks Raymond III continued to seek alliances and support from European powers to bolster his position in the Holy Land.\n\nOne of Raymond III's most notable achievements was his role in the defense of Jerusalem during the 1180s. He supported the efforts of Baldwin IV the Leper King and later participated in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader forces were decisively defeated by Saladin. Despite this defeat Raymond III remained a key figure in the resistance against Muslim forces and played a crucial role in the subsequent efforts to recover the lost territories.\n\n*highlighted section part 3: Later Years and Legacy*\nIn the later years of his life Raymond III focused on securing his counties borders and strengthening his alliances. He married Eschiva of Bures a daughter of his former regents William of Bures and Hodierna in an attempt to heal old wounds and secure his succession. This marriage produced several children including Raymond IV who would succeed him as Count of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 either during the Battle of Hattin or shortly thereafter the exact circumstances of his death remain unclear. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Crusader states in general. Despite the eventual loss of Tripoli to Saladin in 1289 Raymond III's efforts to defend and strengthen his county left a lasting legacy in the history of the Crusades.\n\nAfter his death Raymond III was remembered as a capable and charismatic leader who had done much to preserve the independence and integrity of the County of Tripoli. His reign highlighted the complex interplay of political maneuvering military strategy and diplomatic effort that characterized the Crusader states in the 12th century. Historians continue to study Raymond III's life and actions to understand the broader dynamics of the Crusades and the medieval Near East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035089056473225355}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01817992888391018}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4510277509689331}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09894715994596481}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -1.208800196647644}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.5093410015106201}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -7.390703103737906e-05}, {"token": " lord", "logprob": -0.9644478559494019}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.9059403538703918}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.9625202417373657}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.02794177643954754}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -0.8052737712860107}, {"token": " role", "logprob": -0.01710566319525242}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.1375250816345215}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014939568936824799}, {"token": " history", "logprob": -0.2316630482673645}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.858122211880982e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.01058948040008545}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -1.153239369392395}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.6927575416048057e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.008449164219200611}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.2631302773952484}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.003391349921002984}, {"token": " medieval", "logprob": -1.7213788032531738}, {"token": " period", "logprob": -0.01888018101453781}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.08063293248414993}, {"token": " Born", "logprob": -0.5384321808815002}, {"token": " around", "logprob": -0.5262023210525513}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.22670148313045502}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.1403817855753e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.05167705565690994}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.01704835705459118}, {"